 pretty senior manager  working at the bay report  there was a huge amount of this  fertilizer this chemical that was  brought in it was confiscated it was  kept in a hangar at the port  but it was never disposed of or treated  properly  and left in the port for years ammonium  nitrate  it's highly flammable it's highly  combustible and it's incredibly  dangerous  [Music]  as the day went on this sort of massive  fire was growing and actually because  it was happening at the port it meant  that really across beirut  if you were high enough you'd be able to  see this this huge  plume of smoke that was emanating from  the port  and i was making myself some some dinner  and i got a call from a colleague of  mine at sky  who was just asking me about the general  situation in lebanon  just after christmas 50 of this country  were below the poverty line this is a  city of 2 million people  more than half of the city has been  lebanon for  the best part of a year  had been basically going through this  incredibly  tumultuous period of its history  [Music]  there have been protests across the  country  [Music]  and the epicenter of those protests were  right in the centre of downtown beirut  and this colleague was very keen to know  whether there was perhaps  you know scope to do some stories so i  was talking her through  the general situation in the country and  what was happening  [Music]  and at that moment whilst i was on the  phone i just felt the  ground start to shake  and within a split second there was you  know this  incredible noise  i remember sort of being on the floor  and i could hear  the correspondent who i've been talking  to still  asking me questions on the phone and she  actually said have you dropped the phone  down the toilet  because of the noise because of the sort  of the shock waves  and i just in a sort of split second  picked up the phone and said to i've got  to call you back  there's been a massive explosion  i was working  remotely 40 kilometers  away from the port of baywood  i heard the first explosion  then the second a bigger one  the first thought that came into my mind  is that we are having an israeli strike  in baroque mountain next to us  they used to have strikes on the  telecommunication  antennas there however  five minutes later it was really clear  that there have been some sort of  explosion  in support of baywood  [Music]  we live in cinephil in the suburbs of  beirut  our house was severely damaged by the  blast  we had glass all over the place on our  beds  on our sofas actually first we  thought it was an earthquake  my sister came with her kids and  we turned on the tv the kids were crying  we were trying to concentrate to see  what happened  i called them on the landline and i  asked about my father  we tried calling his phone number his  private number  his work number and we didn't get an  answer  i just went to the to my car and  directly heading i was heading to beirut  i thought myself i have to be open  of what will be happening  my phone didn't stop ringing asking  about my father and i was telling them  my father is there we don't know  any clue of the road i was  asking myself would i go to the poor  or to my family good evening we  start tonight with breaking news there's  been a large explosion in the lebanese  capital beirut it's not known what  caused the blast which centered  on the port area of the city casualties  have been reported  i remember walking out of the apartment  onto a sort of side street that led to  the main square  everything around me was was totally  destroyed  there felt like this kind of orange hue  across the city this kind of  thick orange sort of smog dust  smoke  the sounds of the sirens were incessant  ambulances fire trucks police sirens  were constant i mean constant  the crunching of glass everywhere you  walked  i thought it may have been a car bomb or  some kind of farm or explosion  and i remember at that point is when i  first noticed 